      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "ASK",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — the command typed",
      "narration_text": "In the terminal: ask Claude to write a PyTorch script that loads a pretrained ResNet-50, runs FGSM with epsilon point-zero-one on a sample image, prints the top-three classes and confidence for both original and adversarial, and saves the amplified perturbation as a PNG.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "claude \"Write a Python script using PyTorch + torchvision:\n(1) load pretrained ResNet-50\n(2) run FGSM with epsilon=0.01 on a sample image\n(3) print top-3 classes + confidence:\n    original vs adversarial\n(4) save perturbation amplified 50x as PNG\nFormula: x_adv = x + epsilon * sign(grad_x Loss)\"",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM — ADVERSARIAL ROBUSTNESS",
            "segment": "ADVERSARIAL PERTURBATION DETECTOR",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "generating FGSM attack script…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 16.34,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T16:30:20"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "CODE",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — read the code before running",
      "narration_text": "Read the attack line. x underscore adv equals x plus epsilon times x dot grad dot sign. That sign function is the key: it does not use the full gradient — it uses only which direction to push each pixel. Clipped to the image range. The entire attack is one line, and one backward pass through the loss is all the information it needs.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeCodeBeat",
          "props": {
            "title": "fgsm_attack.py",
            "code": "# fgsm_attack.py  —  Claude Code output\nimport torch\n\ndef fgsm_attack(image, epsilon, data_grad):\n    \"\"\"Fast Gradient Sign Method (Goodfellow et al. 2014)\"\"\"\n    # Collect the sign of the data gradient\n    sign_data_grad = data_grad.sign()\n    # Create the adversarial image: one step in gradient direction\n    perturbed = image + epsilon * sign_data_grad\n    # Clip to valid image range [0, 1]\n    perturbed = torch.clamp(perturbed, 0, 1)\n    return perturbed\n\n# One forward pass + one backward pass = broken classifier\n# x_adv = x + epsilon * sign(grad_x Loss)",
            "sparkLine": "ADVERSARIAL PERTURBATION DETECTOR"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 18.35,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T16:30:20"
      }
    },
    {
